  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "German pronunciation distinguishes long and short vowels. Practice with word pairs like 'ihn - in', 'Beet - Bett', and 'Haus - Maus' helps you hear the difference. Long vowels are held longer than short vowels.\nGerman diphthongs, such as in 'mein', 'Wein', 'nein' and 'leider', are shorter than their English equivalents.\nPost-vocalic 'r' in German sounds vowel-like. Words like 'Haus', 'Maus', 'neu', 'neun', 'deutsch' and 'Leute' illustrate this. When 'r' follows a vowel, the syllable with 'r' is slightly longer and the speaker's mouth opens more. Practice pairs like 'diese - dieser', 'Miete - Mieter', 'Alte - Alter', and 'Vetter - Vettern' to hear the contrast.\nPre-vocalic 'ch' is produced with slight friction between the back of the tongue and the soft palate, similar to the final sound in 'loch' or 'nach'. Practice words such as 'hier', 'sehr', 'Uhr', 'Ohr', 'her - Herr', and 'Bier - wirr' to master this sound.\nThese pronunciation notes emphasize listening carefully to your instructor and practicing regularly with word pairs to develop a native-like sense of vowel length and consonant quality."
    }
